  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "Using percolation analysis across India's 50 largest urban agglomerations from 1975 to 2025, this study measures how separated built-up patches merge. Critical distances required for major settlement-merger events shrank in 47 cities, with the median threshold dropping 46% from 650 m to 350 m. The spatial expansion dynamics invert upon coalescence: footprint growth exceeds built-up surface growth prior to the largest merger (386% vs. 361%), whereas built-up surface expansion surpasses footprint growth immediately following the merger (315% vs. 287%).",
      "why": "It identifies a distinct spatial inflection point in urban growth, demonstrating that the physical coalescence of settlements fundamentally shifts the dominant mode of land consumption from spatial footprint expansion to built-up infill regardless of total population size."
    }
  },
